WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.240
THE STATE OF GOOGLE:
Have you noticed that it’s been quite some

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:06.890
time since Google has hit a homerun. Almost
everything they’ve launched basically since

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:13.389
Chrome back in 2008 has fallen flat of expectations
or been a complete failure. This includes

00:00:13.389 --> 00:00:18.779
Google+, Google Glass, Google Cardboard, Google
Fiber, Google Nexus, Google Allo, and most

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:25.019
recently Google Bard. But, it’s not just
new product launches that aren’t going Google’s

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:31.869
way either. Even their hero products are starting
to lose steam. Many people are switching to

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:36.040
Edge, Bing, and ChatGPT instead of Chrome,
Google, and Bard. More people than ever are

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:41.899
also switching from Gmail to Outlook or even
a third party option like ProtonMail. And

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:46.920
as for Android, well, the iPhone has been
annihilating Android in terms of marketshare

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.760
over the past 10 years. Even YouTube, one
of their golden gooses has started to show

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.420
a few signs of weakness as revenue fell for
3 quarters in a row. Google has been aware

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:04.780
of this shortcoming for quite some time now
and their solution was just brute force. Just

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.840
keep launching new products and entering new
sectors with initiatives like Google TV, Stadia,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.930
and YouTube shorts and hope that something
ends up sticking. But unfortunately, nothing

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:22.240
has really stuck atleast not on the megascale
that you would expect with a giant like Google.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.970
Amongst this graveyard of failures and disappointment
though, there does seem to be one glimmer

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:35.540
of hope: Google Cloud. It looks like Google
has taken a page straight out of Microsoft’s

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:40.010
playbook. When consumer products don’t work
out because of public perception or too much

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:45.869
baggage or whatever, just switch to offering
enterprise products as businesses are far

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:52.690
more logical. They just care about the product
and the price. Your story and ethics is no

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:57.799
concern to them as long as it doesn’t affect
their business. But unlike Microsoft who entered

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:04.100
the cloud scene and made strides in the early
2010s, Google has had an extraordinarily difficult

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.780
time breaking in. In fact, they were losing
billions of dollars trying to chase the cloud

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:15.230
market until the beginning of this year when
the posted their first ever profit. And given

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:20.170
that 50% of all of Google’s current job
openings are for cloud roles, I’d say that

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.510
it’s safe to say that Google is very much
doubling down on the enterprise cloud business.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:30.690
So, join me as we take a look at Google’s
monumental shift from being a ubiquitous consumer

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:37.530
business to being an enterprise juggernaut.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:47.910
EARLY STRUGGLES:
Taking a look back, the story of Google Cloud

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:55.410
takes us back to March of 2006 to launch of
AWS or Amazon Web Services. AWS would eventually

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:00.420
grow to be the gold standard when it comes
to the cloud. Virtually every esteemed company

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:06.560
that you can think of has adopted AWS including
Airbnb, McDonald’s, Twitch, Twitter, Epic

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.880
Games, and even Netflix and Disney. In fact,
some analysts estimate that AWS accounts for

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:17.200
a trillion dollars worth of Amazon’s market
cap. This means that their massive retail

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.730
operation that everyone actually knows about
is only worth a few hundred billion or a mere

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:28.260
fraction of their entire valuation. But when
Amazon launched AWS, they were by no means

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:34.080
trying to create the next big thing. In fact,
the launch of AWS was more of a desparate

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.890
move than a calculated one. Amazon’s retail
business simply wasn’t turning a profit

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:45.100
and with funding drying up due to the dotcom
crash, they needed another source of revenue.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:51.390
Their solution was to simply rent out their
spare servers and databases and this somehow

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:57.319
turned into a trillion dollar business. Google
was watching all of this playout in realtime

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:03.280
and naturally, they felt that they could do
it better. As such, in April of 2008, Google

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.360
would enter the cloud scene with the launch
of App Engine. App Engine had a pretty ambitious

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.340
offer: make it easier than ever for developers
to launch websites by leveraging the power

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:18.199
of Google’s infrastructure. They largely
simplified the process of deploying code and

00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:23.139
managing traffic spikes. In fact, they completely
removed these concerns from the equation as

00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:27.410
they would handle this stuff from their end.
But not only was Google trying to make App

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:34.350
Engine as easy to use as possible but also
as cheap as possible. In fact, the original

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:39.100
version didn’t even have a paid option.
Everyone got a generous free quota to mess

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:44.380
around with and test. And naturally given
that this was from Google, they attracted

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:50.100
a lot of developers, 20,000 to be precise.
One month later, they would open up the service

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:55.639
to all developers and 10 months later, they
would add paid tiers to App Engine but something

00:04:55.639 --> 00:05:01.340
was off. App Engine was by far the easiest
to use and the cheapest especially from a

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:09.970
big tech company but for some reason developers
just weren’t sticking around. This was extremely

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:16.810
confusing. Google had used this same strategy
time and time again usually to great success.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.990
Offering a better service for a cheaper price
or free was literally the bread and butter

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:26.740
of the entirety of Google. So, why in the
world weren’t developers joining up in the

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:32.620
masses? Well, the reality was that Google
had gotten developer community completely

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:38.069
wrong which is rather ironic given that Google
themselves is just a bunch of developers.

00:05:38.069 --> 00:05:42.560
The problem was that in order to make the
service as easy to use as possible, Google

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:48.710
had to sacrifice a lot of customizability.
For Google to deliver on the ease of use aspect,

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:54.350
they had to shrink the infinite developer
landscape into a few succinct boxes because

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:59.430
frankly they could only address so many sectors.
Also, remember how App Engine launched with

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:05.500
only a free tier? Well, that tier was indeed
generous for testing but it was completely

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:11.150
impractical for production applications. Combine
this with CPU limitations, scaling issues,

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:16.560
and slow response times and developers were
out before App Engine was even fully launched.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.430
The OG Google though wasn’t afraid to keep
trying despite failure, so it was only a matter

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:25.900
of time until they put out a new offering.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:36.660
A BREAKING POINT:
A year after the launch of App Engine, Google

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:43.030
would add Java support but this didn’t change
much. As this developer put it “there are

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:47.910
a couple of fundamental issues that must be
addressed, and addressed properly, before

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:53.131
Google App Engine can be taken seriously as
a web application platform. (And no, Java

00:06:53.131 --> 00:07:00.449
support is most definitely not one of them.)”
Alright then, if it’s not java they want,

00:07:00.449 --> 00:07:08.569
maybe it’s cloud storage? No. BigQuery and
Prediction APIs? No. Cloud SQL? No. Ummm,

00:07:08.569 --> 00:07:15.970
24x7 phone support? No. PhP support? No. Encryption?
Nope. None of Google’s additions to their

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:21.990
cloud offering over the next 5 years really
did much to attract the big boys. The bottomline

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:27.750
was that the consumer approach didn’t work
with developers and enterprise customers.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:32.229
Google was trying to offer a watered down
version that was cheap and easy to use when

00:07:32.229 --> 00:07:36.080
developers preferred a fully customizable
option which they were more than happy to

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:42.419
pay for. So ironically, Amazon’s bare bones
offering that infinitely customizable was

00:07:42.419 --> 00:07:49.379
actually far more appealing than Google’s
watered down assortment. This was super frustrating.

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:54.110
Google was literally trying create a developer
offering that was easier and more convenient

00:07:54.110 --> 00:08:00.009
than anything else on the market but for some
reason, developers preferred the less convenient

00:08:00.009 --> 00:08:07.250
stuff. Frustrated, Google would turn back
to what they were good at, the consumer market.

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:13.069
We would see the launch of Google Drive on
April 24, 2012. In classic Google fashion,

00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:20.180
they launched with 15 gb of free storage for
everyone and Drive would take off. Within

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:25.630
3 years, they would grow to 1 million paying
users. By 2017, they would reach a milestone

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:32.820
of storing over 2 trillion files and by 2018,
they would reach 1 billion active users. By

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:39.339
all accounts, Google Drive was a massive success
but Google couldn’t help but feel that they

00:08:39.339 --> 00:08:45.730
had missed out on the larger pie: the enterprise
market. Sure, reaching 1 billion users directly

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:50.860
was great but with a cloud operation like
AWS, they could literally reach trillions

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:56.990
of users across hundreds of ubiquitous platforms.
This feeling was only made worse by Microsoft

00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:03.950
and their surprising success with Azure which
came out years after App Engine. Also, Microsoft

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:08.870
wasn’t even trying to do anything revolutionary
with Azure like Google was. Their entire pitch

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:17.180
was basically just AWS for cheaper. Google
was furious and in 2014, they decided that

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:21.600
if they couldn’t fight their way to the
top, they were gonna buy their way to the

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.480
top. This plan was two fold. On one side,
they would literally buy out smaller cloud

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:31.540
providers and their users like Firebase. On
the other side, they would subsidize their

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:36.740
cloud offerings by a ridiculous amount. In
fact, they would slash prices of all cloud

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:44.740
offerings between 30 and 85%. Surely, this
was a deal that was simply too good to refuse

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:52.180
until customers refused. The harsh reality
was that no company was taking Google’s

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:56.670
offerings seriously. Moreover, they saw no
reason to switch when there were already two

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:02.490
great offerings: AWS and Azure. One that was
focused on performance and one that was focused

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:07.610
on price. Google just didn’t fit in the
scene, and now that they had largely cut prices,

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:13.740
they were burning ridiculous amounts of money.
During the worst of it, they were losing $5.5

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:18.990
billion per year on Cloud alone. Sundar would
desparately try to convince investors that

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:24.750
Google Cloud should be judged based on growth
instead of financials. But investors weren’t

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:30.029
convinced as the growth wasn’t even all
that remarkable compared to the real winners.

00:10:30.029 --> 00:10:34.370
To make things worse, this was the era in
which all of Google’s new consumer offerings

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:42.940
would also start flopping. Google needed change
and they needed it fast. Enter Thomas Kurian.

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:52.190
A GOOGLE MIRACLE:
To be honest, Google got extraordinarily lucky

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:57.080
with Thomas Kurian. If you’re not familiar
with Thomas, that’s understandable as he’s

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:01.820
spent most of his career in the background.
But that isn’t to say that his contributions

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:06.750
were anything short of extraordinary. After
working a few years as a consultant early

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:12.491
on in his career, Thomas joined Oracle in
1996. Over the next 12 years, he would work

00:11:12.491 --> 00:11:17.450
his way all the way up to president of Oracle
reporting directly to Oracle’s founder and

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:23.160
CEO, Larry Ellison. Thomas was like the dream
candidate for Google as he had literally spent

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:28.640
his entire career building up the biggest
enterprise tech company in the world: Oracle.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:33.290
He was in charge of 35,000 developers, deca
billion dollar budgets, and was basically

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:39.450
the de facto CEO of Oracle Cloud. But while
Thomas was the perfect candidate to lead Google

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:45.029
Cloud, Google really had no shot at getting
him. For starters, why would he want to leave

00:11:45.029 --> 00:11:49.529
the world’s most successful enterprise tech
company where he was already the main guy

00:11:49.529 --> 00:11:54.410
to go lead the struggling Google Cloud? It
makes no sense whatsoever. If you’re thinking

00:11:54.410 --> 00:12:00.010
that Google could bribe him with money, this
is also false becuase he has no need for money.

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:04.610
We always talk about how Google is a top paying
employer and how Sundar Pichai is the highest

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:10.480
paid employee in the world but all I can say
is that Thomas has already been there done

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:16.550
that. In fact, Thomas was the world’s 5th
highest paid tech executive way back in 2010

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:23.100
before Sundar was even a thing. And by 2018,
Thomas was earning just under a $100 million.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:29.220
So, at this point, all that was left for Thomas
was to become a C suite executive or even

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:33.410
CEO, potentially earn even more than Sundar
Pichai, work for another 10 years in a familiar

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:39.430
comfortable environment, and retire as an
Oracle legend. But obviously things didn’t

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:44.050
play out that way. That’s why we’re even
talking about Thomas. You see, around the

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:49.010
same time that Google desperately needed help,
Thomas and Larry would get into a massive

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:53.779
argument over the future of Oracle Cloud.
Thomas felt that Oracle should run more of

00:12:53.779 --> 00:13:00.000
their software on AWS and Azure to reach more
customers while Larry felt that they should

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.810
run Oracle software on their own cloud to
bolster the usage of Oracle Cloud. This disagreement

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:11.480
would quickly be blown out of proportions
and on September 28, 2018, Thomas would announce

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:16.139
that he was resigning from Oracle and was
actively searching for new opportunities.

00:13:16.139 --> 00:13:20.870
This was straight up a blessing from god for
Google. During a time in which they needed

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:26.810
more help than ever before, one of them most
qualified cloud executives in the world was

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:31.790
looking for a job. Likely the only people
in the entire world who were more qualified

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:37.800
than Thomas were Andy Jassy, now the CEO of
Amazon, and Mark Russinovich, the CTO of Azure.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:43.120
So, Google lucked one with the 3rd best option
in the entire world out of nowhere. Yeah,

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:47.910
I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear
that Thomas would be hired as Google Cloud

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:55.420
CEO at the start of 2019. But, it was by no
means a smooth road ahead as Thomas was literally

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:59.060
taking over a money pit.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:07.930
A REMARKABLE TURNAROUND:
Thomas says that the main issue at Google

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:12.820
Cloud wasn’t hard to spot once he joined.
The problem was that Google was focused on

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:18.620
creating sophisticated technologies when the
key was build straightforward reliable solutions.

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:23.300
So, the first order of business was shifting
the mindset from “we’re a tech company”

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:28.290
to “we’re a solutions company”. As such,
instead of hiring a bunch of developers, Thomas

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:32.670
would hire a bunch of sales engineers and
solutions architects. These guys are in charge

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:36.010
of engaging with current customers and new
customers and addressing all of their questions

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:40.470
and concerns regarding how Google can accomplish
what they’re hoping for. To really solidify

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:45.269
this mindset shift, Thomas would also acquire
some up and coming cloud companies. His most

00:14:45.269 --> 00:14:50.211
notable acquisitions were Looker for $2.6
billion and Mandiant for $5.4 billion. All

00:14:50.211 --> 00:14:54.790
of these internal changes helped tremendously
in terms of product development and sales

00:14:54.790 --> 00:15:00.690
but likely Thomas’ biggest play was finding
a new distinguishing factor. As we previously

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:06.040
mentioned, AWS is desired for its performance,
Azure is desired for its price, and Google

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:12.690
was trying to be desired for its ease of use.
But what they figured was that developers

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:16.950
don’t actually care about ease of use all
that much if it comes at the cost of performance

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:22.720
or customizability. So, what Google needed
was a new distinguishing factor and that’s

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:29.220
when Thomas stumbled upon the open source
cloud market. Open source platforms like MongoDB

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:34.570
and Elasticsearch were not only having trouble
competing against the likes of AWS but they

00:15:34.570 --> 00:15:39.920
were actively being attacked by the likes
of AWS and Azure. Seeing this, Thomas knew

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:45.620
exactly what their distinguishing factor would
be. They would be the champion of open source

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:52.050
cloud solutions. With that, he would form
a partnership with 7 of the most popular opensource

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:56.560
solutions. This gave developers the tremendous
power of the Google infrastructure as well

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:02.380
as the insane freedom and customization offered
by these opensource platforms. And that was

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:07.029
it. That was the turning point which finally
started attracting large clients including

00:16:07.029 --> 00:16:12.850
Coinbase, Deutsche Bank, Ford, General Mills,
and SpaceX. In fact, Google Cloud is now performing

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:18.089
even better than their ad business which has
become stagnant. Google Cloud now also accounts

00:16:18.089 --> 00:16:22.840
for ¼ of Google’s entire workforce and
judging by their open roles, they’re only

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:27.529
looking to grow this bigger. And speaking
of growing bigger, they have plenty of space

00:16:27.529 --> 00:16:33.139
do so as they still only control about 10%
of the market. All of this brings us back

00:16:33.139 --> 00:16:36.990
to Google’s original issue which is that
none of their recent consumer products have

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:42.130
been sticking and their existing ones are
starting to lose steam. This is not a good

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:47.410
omen for any company but luckily for Google,
it seems like they have built a solution to

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:52.300
grow past their consumer days just like Microsoft
did a decade ago. So, while Google’s future

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:57.420
in the consumer market is looking bleak, their
future in the enterprise market is looking

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:03.490
brighter than ever and that’s why the Cloud
is the future of Google. Speaking of consumer

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:08.839
products that are losing steam, ChatGPT’s
accuracy has really fallen off a cliff. Check

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:13.400
out this video to learn more.

